--TEST--
`Core\Db\DbError` is a class in the `Throwable` tree under `RuntimeError`, caught by its own name, by its parent's and by the root's, and never by the sibling `Core\Db\RolledBack` — `rule:core-classes/db-error`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:core-classes/db-error`: one class for every refusal the database itself made, rather
// than one class per condition — the condition is a normalised `kind`.
try {
    throw new Core\Db\DbError("duplicate key value violates a unique constraint");
} catch (Core\Db\DbError $duplicate) {
    echo "own: ", $duplicate->message, "\n";
}

// It sits under `RuntimeError`: the world said no. A handler written
// for that reading catches it without naming the database at all.
try {
    throw new Core\Db\DbError("the connection was reset");
} catch (RuntimeError $reset) {
    echo "parent: ", $reset->message, "\n";
}

// And the root, which is what a `catch` meaning "anything at all" spells.
try {
    throw new Core\Db\DbError("deadlock detected");
} catch (Throwable $deadlock) {
    echo "root: ", $deadlock->message, "\n";
}

// `DbError` and `RolledBack` are siblings, not one name: a handler for the rollback the
// program asked for does not swallow a refusal it never chose. `rule:core-classes/db-transactions` is
// the other half.
try {
    throw new Core\Db\DbError("canceling statement due to statement timeout");
} catch (Core\Db\RolledBack $rolled) {
    echo "sibling: ", $rolled->reason, "\n";
} catch (Core\Db\DbError $refused) {
    echo "not the sibling\n";
}
--EXPECT--
own: duplicate key value violates a unique constraint
parent: the connection was reset
root: deadlock detected
not the sibling
